2026-02-04 19:02:37 [scudmarks@discord] every other model I test ends up in endless loop with my current tool calling implementation, any suggestions?
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] Based on the tool handling patterns in `slopfountain.ts`, here are concrete fixes for infinite tool
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] loops:
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] 1. Enforce Depth Limits
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] Your `relay()` function tracks depth but likely doesn't cap it. Bail out after 3-5 levels:
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:00 [kimi-k2.5] async function relay(depth:number) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     if (depth > 3) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]         echoFail("Tool recursion limit reached");
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]         return 0; // Stop here
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     // ... existing logic
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     const spent = await relay(depth + 1); // Recursive call
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Disable Tools After First Use
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] Most loops happen when models chain tool calls indefinitely. Force-disable tools after the first
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] tool cycle:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] const useTools = grokFunctions && roha.config.tools && depth === 0;
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] // Only allow tools on depth 0, not in recursive calls
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] Or toggle off after any tool execution:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] if (calls) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     grokFunctions = false; // Disable for next turn
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     await writeForge();
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Validate Tool Result Formatting
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] Loops often occur when the model can't correlate results with calls. Ensure your `processToolCalls`
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] returns exactly:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     tool_call_id: tool.id,  // Must match the call ID exactly
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     name: tool.function.name,
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     content: JSON.stringify(result)
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] And push to history as:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] rohaHistory.push({
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     role: "tool",  // or "system" with tool_call_id
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     tool_call_id: id,
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     name: name,
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     content: content
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] });
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 4. History Pollution Check
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] If your history shows duplicate assistant messages with the same `tool_calls`, the model sees stale
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] context. Squash sequences:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] function squashMessages(history) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     // Ensure assistant messages with tool_calls aren't duplicated
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     // before adding new tool results
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 5. Model-Specific Disabling
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] Some models (DeepSeek, older GPTs) loop more than others. Your code already has pattern
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] detection—extend it:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] if (line.includes("maximum prompt length") ||
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     line.includes("infinite_loop_detected")) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     grokFunctions = false;
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     return spend;
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 6. One Tool Per Turn
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] Restrict payload to single tool execution:
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] if (calls && calls.length > 0) {
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     // Only process first call, ignore rest
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     const tool = calls[0];
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5]     // ... process single tool
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] The key insight in your codebase: `relay(depth+1)` creates the recursion opportunity. Without a
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] depth check or tool-disable mechanism, models that "think" they need to keep calling tools will
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] loop forever.
2026-02-04 19:03:01 [kimi-k2.5] 
