2026-02-04 19:12:31 [scudmarks@discord] history of  ℘ power-set script p - go deep
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The ℘ symbol (U+2118, Unicode "SCRIPT CAPITAL P") carries one of typography's great
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] mathematical identity crises—simultaneously representing Weierstrass's elliptic function and, by
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] convergent evolution, the power set operation.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Weierstrass Genesis (1850s)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Karl Weierstrass introduced ℘(z) in his theory of elliptic functions around 1856-1860. The
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] symbol is specifically not a standard script P—it's a stylized lowercase German 'p'
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] (p-Function) with a distinctive looped bowl and extended ascender. Weierstrass chose it to
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] distinguish his p-function from Legendre's earlier elliptic function notation.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] • Critical distinction: In proper mathematical typography, ℘ is not the power set
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] symbol. It is the Weierstrass elliptic function*.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The Power Set Collision (1930s-1970s)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The power set 𝒫(S)—the set of all subsets—traditionally used:
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - 𝒫 (U+1D4AB, Mathematical Script Capital P, added Unicode 3.1)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Or simply "P" in fraktur/script contexts
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The collision occurred because:
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 1. Limited typewriter/composer fonts (1950s-70s) often included ℘ (Weierstrass) but not 𝒫
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] (proper script P)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 2. TeX (Knuth, 1978) originally mapped `\wp` to ℘ for Weierstrass, but early users
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] repurposed it for power sets when `\mathcal{P}` rendered inconsistently
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Unicode 1.0 (1991) included U+2118 as "SCRIPT CAPITAL P" (general category: Symbol, not
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Letter), cementing the dual usage
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Unicode Schism
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] • U+2118 ℘* (Weierstrass p):
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Original name: "SCRIPT CAPITAL P" (misnomer—it's lowercase in origin)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Category: Symbol (So)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Font designers render it with the distinctive Weierstrass loop
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] • U+1D4AB 𝒫* (Mathematical Script Capital P):
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Added Unicode 3.1 (2001) specifically for power sets
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Category: Letter, Uppercase (Lu)
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Part of the Mathematical Alphanumeric Symbols block
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Detection in Slop Fountain
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Your `slopfountain.ts` handles this ambiguity in the `stringWidth()` functions:
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] // ℘ is handled as double-width in some contexts, single in others
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] // depending on whether it's treated as Symbol or Letter
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The `discordStringWidth()` function likely miscounts ℘ as width 1 when it's actually 2 in most
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] monospace Unicode contexts, or vice versa depending on normalization.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Modern Resolution
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - ISO 80000-2 (2009) specifies 𝒫 (U+1D4AB) for power sets
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - AMS-LaTeX uses `\wp` strictly for Weierstrass, `\mathcal{P}` or `\mathfrak{P}` for power
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] sets
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] - Programming languages: Python, Julia, and Haskell occasionally use ℘ in math libraries
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] for either meaning—context determines semantics
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] The symbol remains a gestalt ambiguity in mathematical computing: typographically the
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] Weierstrass function, semantically overloaded by decades of limited character sets.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] • In slop fountain context*: If you're generating mathematical content, the
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] `texToUnicode()` function should probably map `\wp` → ℘ (Weierstrass) and `\mathcal{P}` → 𝒫 (Power
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] set), though many legacy texts conflate them.
2026-02-04 19:12:57 [kimi-k2.5] 
