2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] • Linux/Unix Systems (syslog standard):*
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - EMERG (0): System is unusable
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - ALERT (1): Action must be taken immediately
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - CRIT (2): Critical conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - ERR (3): Error conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - WARNING (4): Warning conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - NOTICE (5): Normal but significant conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - INFO (6): Informational messages
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - DEBUG (7): Debug-level messages
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] • Windows Event Log:*
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - Critical (1): Highest severity
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - Error (2): Error conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - Warning (3): Warning conditions
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - Information (4): Informational messages
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] - Verbose (5): Detailed debugging information
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] • Main differences:*
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 1. Linux uses numeric levels 0-7, Windows uses named levels
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 2. Linux has more granularity (8 levels vs Windows' 5)
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 3. Linux separates by facility (kern, user, mail, etc.), Windows by source
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 4. Linux logs to text files, Windows to binary event logs
2026-02-21 09:48:34 [deepseek-chat] 
