2026-02-21 09:49:26 [nitro@ryzen5] in python is there a print command that takes a log level to decide when it is output?
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] In standard Python, there's no built-in `print()` command that takes a log level. However, Python
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] has a comprehensive logging module that provides exactly this functionality.
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] • Basic example with logging levels:*
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] import logging
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] # Configure logging
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] # Messages with different levels
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.debug("Debug message - only shown if level is DEBUG or lower")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.info("Info message - shown if level is INFO or lower")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.warning("Warning message - shown if level is WARNING or lower")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.error("Error message - shown if level is ERROR or lower")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.critical("Critical message - always shown")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] • Setting different output levels:*
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] import logging
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] # Only show WARNING and above
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.basicConfig(level=logging.WARNING)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.debug("This won't appear")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.info("This won't appear")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logging.warning("This WILL appear")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] • Custom logger with level filtering:*
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] import logging
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] # Create handler with specific level
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] handler = logging.StreamHandler()
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] handler.setLevel(logging.INFO)  # Only INFO and above
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logger.addHandler(handler)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logger.debug("Debug - filtered out")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] logger.info("Info - appears")
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] • Logging levels in Python (from lowest to highest severity):*
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 1. `NOTSET` (0)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 2. `DEBUG` (10)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 3. `INFO` (20)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 4. `WARNING` (30)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 5. `ERROR` (40)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 6. `CRITICAL` (50)
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] The logging module is much more powerful than `print()` - it supports different handlers (file,
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] console, network), formatting, filtering by level, and is the standard way to handle logging in
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] production Python applications.
2026-02-21 09:49:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:34 [nitro@ryzen5] in deno typescript?
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] In Deno TypeScript, you have several options for logging with levels:
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] • 1. Deno's built-in `log` module:*
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] import { log } from "https://deno.land/std@0.200.0/log/mod.ts";
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] // Setup logger with level
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] await log.setup({
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   handlers: {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     console: new log.handlers.ConsoleHandler("DEBUG"),
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   },
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   loggers: {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     default: {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]       level: "DEBUG",
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]       handlers: ["console"],
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     },
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   },
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] });
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] const logger = log.getLogger();
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] // Log with different levels
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.debug("Debug message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.info("Info message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.warning("Warning message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.error("Error message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.critical("Critical message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] • 2. Using console with custom wrapper:*
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] enum LogLevel {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   DEBUG = 0,
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   INFO = 1,
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   WARN = 2,
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   ERROR = 3,
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   CRITICAL = 4
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] class Logger {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   private level: LogLevel;
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   constructor(level: LogLevel = LogLevel.INFO) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     this.level = level;
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   debug(...args: unknown[]) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     if (this.level <= LogLevel.DEBUG) console.debug(...args);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   info(...args: unknown[]) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     if (this.level <= LogLevel.INFO) console.info(...args);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   warn(...args: unknown[]) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     if (this.level <= LogLevel.WARN) console.warn(...args);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   error(...args: unknown[]) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     if (this.level <= LogLevel.ERROR) console.error(...args);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   critical(...args: unknown[]) {
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]     console.error("CRITICAL:", ...args);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat]   }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] // Usage
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] const logger = new Logger(LogLevel.DEBUG);
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.debug("Debug message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] logger.info("Info message");
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] • 3. Popular third-party libraries:*
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - Winston: `import winston from "npm:winston"`
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - Pino: `import pino from "npm:pino"`
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - Bunyan: `import bunyan from "npm:bunyan"`
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] • Deno std log levels:*
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - `DEBUG` (10)
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - `INFO` (20)
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - `WARNING` (30)
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - `ERROR` (40)
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] - `CRITICAL` (50)
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] The Deno standard library's `log` module is the most common choice as it's built specifically for
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] Deno and provides structured logging with configurable levels, handlers, and formatters.
2026-02-21 09:50:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:10 [nitro@ryzen5] does ts have enum?
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] Yes, TypeScript has enums! TypeScript provides several types of enums:
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • 1. Numeric enums (default):*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum Direction {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Up = 1,    // 1
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Down,      // 2 (auto-incremented)
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Left,      // 3
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Right      // 4
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum LogLevel {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   DEBUG,     // 0 (default starts at 0)
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   INFO,      // 1
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   WARN,      // 2
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   ERROR,     // 3
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   CRITICAL   // 4
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • 2. String enums:*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum LogLevel {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   DEBUG = "DEBUG",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   INFO = "INFO",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   WARN = "WARN",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   ERROR = "ERROR",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   CRITICAL = "CRITICAL"
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • 3. Heterogeneous enums (mixed string and numeric - not recommended):*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum Mixed {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   No = 0,
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Yes = "YES"
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • 4. Const enums (compile-time only, no runtime object):*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] const enum Direction {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Up = "UP",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Down = "DOWN"
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] // Compiles to just the values, no runtime object
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • Usage examples:*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum Status {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Active = "ACTIVE",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Inactive = "INACTIVE",
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Pending = "PENDING"
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] function setStatus(status: Status) {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(`Setting status to: ${status}`);
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] setStatus(Status.Active);  // OK
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] setStatus("ACTIVE");       // Error: Type '"ACTIVE"' is not assignable to type 'Status'
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] // Reverse mapping (numeric enums only)
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] enum Response {
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   No = 0,
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat]   Yes = 1
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] }
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] console.log(Response.Yes);    // 1
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] console.log(Response[1]);     // "Yes" (reverse mapping)
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] console.log(Response["Yes"]); // 1
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] • Key points:*
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] - Enums create both a type and a runtime object
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] - String enums don't have reverse mapping
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] - Const enums are removed at compile time (better performance)
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] - Enums are great for fixed sets of related constants
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] TypeScript enums are more powerful than JavaScript's object-based approaches because they provide
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] type safety and can be used as both types and values.
2026-02-21 09:51:20 [deepseek-chat] 
