2026-02-24 16:04:22 [nitro@ryzen5] how to use env var in bat file
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] In a `.bat` file, wrap the variable name in percent signs:
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] echo %USERNAME%
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] • Set temporary (session only):*
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] set MY_VAR=hello
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] echo %MY_VAR%
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] • Use with commands:*
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] set PORT=8081
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] node server.js --port %PORT%
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] • Check if defined:*
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] if defined MY_VAR (
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5]     echo %MY_VAR%
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] ) else (
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5]     echo not set
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] )
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] Note: Variables set with `set` persist only for that command window. Use `setx` to persist
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] permanently (requires new window to see the change).
2026-02-24 16:04:31 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:06:01 [nitro@ryzen5] line continuation in bat file
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] Batch files use the caret `^` for line continuation. Place it at the end of each line to be
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] continued:
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] command ^
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 	argument1 ^
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 	argument2
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] Since batch cannot natively listen on TCP ports, you would typically wrap PowerShell or use `ncat`.
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] Would you like a reusable batch function that launches a PowerShell listener on port 8081?
2026-02-24 16:07:11 [kimi-k2.5] 
